From 259627f2d7f6fe34c9f22a74da4a92b35c55b890 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pdale-boop <237306950+pdale-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 16:12:10 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 192/192] Aurora: the scene's brightness from the game, where
 there is depth and not the sea

aurora_set_exposure(gain) brightens the scene after the HD look's effects and
before its bloom: kBoost, scene * (1 + gain - 1), where there is depth and not
the sea. BlueWake sends Wind Waker HD's own factor for the hour's palettes (its
sunlight 3 times at noon on actors and scenery, twice in the morning and
evening, 1.65 and 1.37 after gamma). Off (0 or 1) the pass is left out. The
gain rides in the pass's copy of the view's seconds, which it doesn't read.

HD brightens the sun's light, not the sea: one gain over the whole scene
washed the sea out to a pale cyan before the deck was bright enough, and over
the sky it took the floating clouds to a clipped white.

The sea is found by a mask, not by height: a band above the sea's height left
beaches below a swell's height unlit and lit the swells' crests. The scene
passes' draws go again into an 8-bit mask with a depth buffer of its own, the
sea's marking it and the rest that write depth covering it. A sea draw is one
that samples the sea's textures, by the texture packs' hash of their bytes:
Always.arc's b_sea_tex0and2 (d_a_sea's water and foam lines), the island
rooms' ring of sea (Txa_umi_kiwa_01, Txa_umi_kage_01) and their beaches' surf
(Txa_nami_01, Txa_nami_02, Txa_nami_ia, Txa_namisoko_ia), and still and running
water (the Private Oasis's pool, Txa_dummy_umi; the rivers' and falls' edge,
flow and spray, Txa_test_kiwa_a, _nagare_j, _sibuki_a); or the sea rooms'
untextured square of sea past the grid's edge (solid, 100,000 units across and
flat). What covers the sea there is what the sun map takes as solid: a
blended draw doesn't, and an alpha-tested one only where its texture is solid
(a wave's small crest, whole, covered a box of sea that was then brightened).
DOL_AURORA_SEA_MASK_DEBUG=1 shows the mask: the sea twice as bright.

The brightness fades out from 10,000 to 25,000 units away
(DOL_AURORA_EXPOSURE_FAR=start,end): what is that far is the haze's colour,
and brightened, the islands seen from the Private Oasis stood out pale cyan
against the sky.

What is already bright takes less of the gain: it tapers from full where a
colour's brightest channel is 0.4 to none at 1.0
(DOL_AURORA_EXPOSURE_HIGHLIGHTS=from,to), gently enough that a brighter colour
still comes out brighter. At 1.5 times, Ice Ring Isle's ice and white rock
went flat white, a sixth of the screen clipped. The brightness is read from
the frame buffer copied before the effects (a shadow, read after them, took
more of the gain than the lit ice beside it and came out washed out).

Arch (Vulkan), Windfall's dock from the stage select with HD's palettes,
against Wind Waker HD in Cemu, 23 times from 4:00 to midnight: the deck,
cliffs and sea within about 10% of HD's at most hours (noon's deck 203,160,99
against 217,173,96, the sea 9,148,240 against 0,140,227). The mask, in its
debug view and against the height band: a beach and swells at sea room 10,
Dragon Roost's shore (the surf and the wet band under it), the open sea seen
from high above at the Forsaken Fortress and Hyrule Castle (no sea, nothing
marked). Sailing from sea room 10 into Windfall's room: the crests' boxes
gone. Warped to the Private Oasis at noon: its pool deep blue as
on the GameCube (it was pale cyan) and the far islands in the haze. Warped to Ice Ring Isle at noon: the ice keeps its
shading, almost none of it clipped (2.5% of the screen before).
